Fillets and chamfers: a corner's sharp edge is limited only by a common
point that lies on it (mitcad#1005).

The case: an L bracket, a floor with a wall at its back; the floor's top
edge along one end (convex) and its edge along the wall (concave) are
chamfered 1. At their common vertex the wall's vertical edge goes on up,
sharp. PerformTwoCornerbyInter finds the two bevels' common points on
different arcs and hands the corner to PerformMoreThreeCorner, which takes
it as OnSame + OnDiff: PerformOneCorner(prepare) moves the convex bevel's
OnSame common point from the floor's far vertical edge, where its contact
ran to, onto the prolongation of the wall's sharp edge below the vertex,
keeping the arc and the parameter of the far edge. The plate's limit on
the sharp edge is then taken from the bevels' two common points beside it,
the nearer to the vertex, cp2 of equal distances; both lie 1 from the
vertex, and at one end of the bracket rounding made the moved point the
nearer. Its parameter on the far edge (36, measured from the wall's top)
was read on the sharp edge's curve: the plate was bounded 31 above the
floor on the wall, a B-spline of degree 8 over 122 mm2 waving across the
bracket's end, and the body was 16.6 mm3 short there. Meshed for display at
0.01 mm, that face took 86 000 triangles and 4 s (8 s and more under load)
where the same corner at the other end takes about a thousand triangles and
a few milliseconds.

- PerformMoreThreeCorner, where a sharp edge is limited by a common point
  (ParameterOnSharpEdge): a common point counts only where it lies on that
  edge, with its parameter on its arc where the arc is the edge, otherwise
  with that of its point projected on the edge within the point's
  tolerance, between the edge's ends. A common point on another arc no
  longer gives the edge a parameter of that arc's curve.
- Of two common points at equal distances, cp2 is taken as before unless
  it lies on another arc and cp1 on the edge: then cp1. The bracket's two
  ends, either edge given first, now get the same corner: the convex
  bevel's end 1 before the wall, the concave bevel's reaching the end face, a
  plate of 1.85 mm2 between them, and the body within 0.03 mm3 of the two
  bevels' prisms at each corner.

Corners whose common points both lie on the sharp edge are unchanged.

Upstream: new.
